WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:06.560
"The Tomorrow War" is thought-provoking sci-fi&nbsp;
at its best. As with any sci-fi this ambitious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:10.320
a few questions remain unanswered&nbsp;
once the credits roll. Here are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:13.600
just some of the questions to ponder&nbsp;
after watching "The Tomorrow War."

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:18.240
While time travel can be fickle&nbsp;
and messy, it's also intricate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:22.800
and unfathomable. If humans can figure&nbsp;
out a way to time travel, then surely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:28.080
higher-tech weapons would be available for the&nbsp;
humanity-defining war against extraterrestrials.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:32.960
Dan Forester, played by Chris Pratt, and his&nbsp;
fellow soldiers are given guns that seemingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:38.400
fire modern-day bullets, then they're thrown into&nbsp;
battle to fight an enemy they know nothing about.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:43.520
Tasked with eradicating formidable aliens&nbsp;
multiplying at an unimaginable rate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:48.480
future humans are outnumbered and using weapons&nbsp;
that seem like they're throwing pebbles at an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:53.600
attacking animal. Luckily, Future Murik is&nbsp;
a genius and comes up with a toxin to kill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:57.920
the aliens, and Dan takes the toxin back&nbsp;
to the past, locating the creatures when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:01.840
they lie dormant and killing them before&nbsp;
the war can take place in the future.

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:07.440
So if the future can build a wormhole, surely&nbsp;
it can create better weapons? The conscripted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:11.360
soldiers and civilians are expected to&nbsp;
believe that time travel exists, while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:16.560
at the same time accepting that military-grade&nbsp;
weaponry uses the same ammo thirty years later.

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:22.400
In the future, the global population is in&nbsp;
trouble because of the war against aliens.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:27.120
The future responds by recruiting soldiers from&nbsp;
the past, but not every person who's drafted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:32.400
has military experience, or even knows how to&nbsp;
load a gun. The only qualification appears to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:38.160
be that draftees have to have already died&nbsp;
by 2051, the year of the war. Two of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:43.120
same people can't exist at the same time, we&nbsp;
are told, or else a time paradox will ensue.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:49.440
"We are you. 30 years in the future."

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:55.600
The future has somehow created a machine that&nbsp;
can determine if and when someone died. Oddly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:01.360
Muri Forester exists in both timelines. She's a&nbsp;
child in the present and an adult in the future.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:06.000
But why is Muri the only character that&nbsp;
naturally appears in the present and future?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.160
All the present-day characters that appear in&nbsp;
the future are dead. All the future people can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:14.480
appear in the past because they have yet to&nbsp;
be born, which keeps the time paradox at bay.

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:19.760
On the one hand, the world's population, which&nbsp;
is currently about 7.6 billion in real life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:25.120
is down to half a million, which means only&nbsp;
a tiny sliver of humans are still alive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:29.760
And sure, you can probably surmise that other&nbsp;
present-soldiers' children might be among the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:34.400
future residents. On the other hand, Muri&nbsp;
being the only character who appears on both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:38.960
ends of the time-jumping seems odd,&nbsp;
almost as if it's not a coincidence.

00:02:40.365 --> 00:02:43.920
Future Muri Forester becomes a&nbsp;
scientist in honor of her father.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:46.400
In the future, she's the world's last hope.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:51.120
However, since we watch Dan successfully lead&nbsp;
a mission to kill the aliens once he returns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:56.240
to the past, does Muri's life trajectory&nbsp;
change? The aliens supposedly no longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:01.040
invade Earth. Which likely means that although&nbsp;
Muri wanted to follow in her dad's footsteps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:06.160
she will no longer grow up with the same sense of&nbsp;
urgency to become a scientist and save the world.

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:10.400
"If there's one thing the world&nbsp;
needs right now, it's scientists."

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:16.240
Since any change during time travel can&nbsp;
have a catastrophic effect on the timeline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:20.560
there's no guarantee that Dan grows apart&nbsp;
from his family, dies in a car accident,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:25.440
or inspires his daughter to save the world.&nbsp;
Which, actually, might all be good things.

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:31.600
After Dan is drafted to fight in the war and the&nbsp;
military places a device on his arm, the military&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:36.960
makes it clear that his wife will have to take his&nbsp;
place if he decides to run. Dodging the draft is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:42.960
a major offense, even in a sci-fi movie like "The&nbsp;
Tomorrow War." That said, when Dan asks his father&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:48.400
James if he can take the device off his arm, this&nbsp;
appears to be something he does with regularity.

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:52.720
How does the old man pull this off? Shouldn't&nbsp;
the government have detected the devices being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:58.160
taken off other people? Whom do the devices belong&nbsp;
to? Why does James have them? While he seems like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:03.120
a crafty person who lives off the grid, the movie&nbsp;
dances around the fact that he has collected these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:08.480
arm devices, despite the military telling Dan that&nbsp;
if he runs or takes off the device, they'll find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:13.760
him. That all makes it sound as if the consequence&nbsp;
is inevitable. Not to mention, these devices must&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:18.480
be super high-tech, as they help enable a&nbsp;
person to transport to and from the future.

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:23.680
When the military is testing Dan to see&nbsp;
if he's an adequate soldier for the war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:29.040
a Certificate of Death is revealed that says&nbsp;
he'll die at age 47, seven years from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:33.840
present. Because of this realization, Dan&nbsp;
is considered a match for the future war.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:38.000
Since it's implied that past events&nbsp;
change the future, what happens to Dan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:43.760
after the war is stopped? Does he still die in&nbsp;
seven years? Also unexplained is what made him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:47.920
become such a different person, becoming&nbsp;
unhappy with his life, divorcing his wife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:53.040
and doing exactly what his father did to him, by&nbsp;
leaving his family behind. His daughter reveals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:57.680
all this information, but never delves into the&nbsp;
motivations that made them happen beyond saying

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:02.800
"You just, you never seemed happy with your life."

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:09.280
Since "The Tomorrow War" ends on a happy note,&nbsp;
it's assumed that Dan rewrote his future. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:13.840
won't abandon his family, and he won't get&nbsp;
into an accident that leads to his death.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:17.520
Although, how do you avoid an&nbsp;
inevitable car accident? Just stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:21.520
driving? Would life eventually make it&nbsp;
happen anyway, "Final Destination"-style?

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:26.720
What we do know is that Dan's death certificate&nbsp;
was created, and the date of that event is still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:32.160
seven years in the future. That said, Dorian's&nbsp;
story was rewritten. Instead of dying of cancer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:36.160
he died while blowing up the alien&nbsp;
ship. So, maybe Dan doesn't die in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:40.160
a car accident, but instead Stuck in the present
lands that dream science job he's always wanted?

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:45.840
People from the future are young in the present&nbsp;
timeline because, as Dan and Charlie conclude,

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:49.520
"They haven't been born yet."

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:54.640
However, when the wormhole closes, it appears&nbsp;
that future soldiers are stuck in the present&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.640 --> 00:05:59.920
as the portal can't be reopened. This is why the&nbsp;
only way to stop the war from occurring is to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:04.880
where the aliens are hiding during the present&nbsp;
day. So, are future soldiers stuck in the past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:09.600
even though they've never been born? Wouldn't&nbsp;
that wreak havoc on innumerable timelines?

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:13.360
"The jump link just went offline,&nbsp;
we can't send anything back."

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:18.640
Time in this universe is like a river&nbsp;
that flows in one steady direction. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:22.720
the jump link places two rafts on this&nbsp;
river, which are thirty years apart.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:26.560
Both rafts continue to travel along&nbsp;
the river, time continues to pass,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:30.400
yet the jump link only allows people&nbsp;
to jump from one raft to the other.

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:34.720
"Time only flows in one&nbsp;
direction. It's like a river."

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:39.520
Although this concept explains why people&nbsp;
can't time travel to different periods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:43.120
and can only travel to the period&nbsp;
where each raft is located,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:47.840
it doesn't explain how unborn people can get&nbsp;
stuck in the past, when the future they came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:53.680
from no longer happens. "The Tomorrow War" is&nbsp;
careful not to create a time paradox, but it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:59.280
avoids this question. Once Dan stops the aliens&nbsp;
from attacking, the future is presumably erased.

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:03.040
People can't live in both timelines at&nbsp;
the same time. Presumably, not all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:07.920
future soldiers die as only a handful were&nbsp;
part of Dan's mission. So, can the unborn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:12.720
soldiers who survived be born again in the&nbsp;
future? Wouldn't that create a time paradox?

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:18.640
Can the wormhole open back up? If it can,&nbsp;
what will the future look like? Can the unborn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:23.280
soldiers return to their correct timelines?&nbsp;
The future should still exist in this reality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:28.080
since the portal simply closed, and it can&nbsp;
presumably be opened again. Or does the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:32.880
that it closed mean the future is lost forever&nbsp;
and there is only one raft left on the river?

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:37.760
There's plenty of reasons why they can't make&nbsp;
a new one immediately. The jump links are 30&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:42.880
years apart and keep moving forward. You&nbsp;
can only jump from one raft to the other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:48.000
More rafts can't be built because they're held&nbsp;
together poorly. The future barely managed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:52.800
make one rudimentary wormhole. If they weren't&nbsp;
about to be extinct, they'd still be testing it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:58.320
with lab rats. All that aside, now that there&nbsp;
isn't a global threat, surely the scientists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:03.440
will have more time to understand the wormhole,&nbsp;
make it safer, more reliable, and expand on it.

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:08.640
This wormhole helped save the world, so that's&nbsp;
not a technology that sound minds would forget.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:12.720
It seems quite likely that the remaining&nbsp;
people in the future could resurrect the tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:16.720
and once again connect to the past. If&nbsp;
these people are intelligent enough to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:20.080
create a wormhole in the first place,&nbsp;
they should be able to do it again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:23.040
and this time, perhaps add&nbsp;
more destinations as well.

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:27.600
Thanks to Dan's volcano-loving&nbsp;
student in the present timeline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:32.240
his crew locates where the aliens are&nbsp;
hiding. As it turns out, the aliens have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:36.640
been here the whole time and dug upwards to&nbsp;
the surface after being frozen for about a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:41.760
thousand years. They are due to complete this&nbsp;
process at some point around 2048 in Russia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:46.800
so Dan and his crew must preempt that by&nbsp;
ambushing them in the present day. To do so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:52.480
they fly to Russia undetected, which seems like an&nbsp;
implausible feat given that, in the film, humanity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:57.360
is on the edge of collapse and rioting in the&nbsp;
streets in the belief death will be coming soon.

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:02.320
James might be a very talented pilot. But&nbsp;
seriously, how did he fly a plane into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:07.440
Russia without a single person detecting it on&nbsp;
the radar? His previously-established skills&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:11.440
in staying off the grid and helping others do&nbsp;
the same is supposed to make this believable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:16.960
but it still seems to be a stretch. "The Tomorrow&nbsp;
War" is a film about heroes who find a way to win&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:21.840
at all costs, but this heroism seems like it&nbsp;
had a little movie magic sprinkled on top.

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:24.000
"Roger that."

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:29.920
After Dan's crew locates the alien ship&nbsp;
in Russia, they break into it and discover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:34.080
two dead pilot creatures hanging from&nbsp;
the ceiling that aren't white spikes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:36.560
but they certainly aren't humans either.

00:09:36.560 --> 00:09:40.960
Unlike the aliens they have been battling,&nbsp;
which are carnivorous and only appear to eat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:46.160
and reproduce, the pilot creatures appear more&nbsp;
advanced. They're capable of building and flying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:51.520
a ship and, it appears, keeping the white spikes&nbsp;
safe in some sort of preserving goo. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:55.920
they weren't that good at flying, however,&nbsp;
as they most likely crash-landed on Earth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.920 --> 00:10:00.240
Perhaps these creatures created the&nbsp;
white spikes or are a mutation of them?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:02.160
The audience will probably never know.

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:08.160
When it comes to the white spikes, countless&nbsp;
questions remain. How did the spaceship crash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:13.600
in Russia? What were they looking for? What were&nbsp;
they running from? Was Earth even their target?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:17.440
Humans have long been obsessed with&nbsp;
possible life out there in the universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:22.400
and "The Tomorrow War" firmly says it exists.&nbsp;
But the more you speculate about such things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.400 --> 00:10:25.200
the more follow-up questions present themselves.

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:29.760
The aliens may have been looking for resources,&nbsp;
as they treated our world like an all-you-can-eat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:34.480
buffet. But what would they have done once&nbsp;
they ate every last human and animal? Remember,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:39.520
it only took them three years to have us on the&nbsp;
verge of extinction, so these guys are hungry.

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:43.840
Perhaps most importantly, are we really&nbsp;
sure every last white spike was killed?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.840 --> 00:10:48.240
Visibility in that snow was pretty bad, and&nbsp;
blowing up their ship doesn't guarantee their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:53.600
extinction from Earth. If the female white spike&nbsp;
was temporarily able to escape, who's to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.600 --> 00:10:58.640
another one didn't unknowingly flee? Who's to say&nbsp;
there wasn't another female white spike on board,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.640 --> 00:11:04.160
or even worse, a second ship buried elsewhere,&nbsp;
perfectly giving us the premise for a sequel?

00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:08.240
Perhaps the biggest unanswered&nbsp;
question from "The Tomorrow War"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:10.880
is what other life exists in this universe?

00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:15.280
The white spikes have tentacles that shoot&nbsp;
spikes, fangs that chew through human flesh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.280 --> 00:11:20.080
like soft cheese, and an exterior virtually&nbsp;
immune to bullets. They are predators,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.080 --> 00:11:25.040
and humans weren't their first meals, which&nbsp;
implies that they eat other creatures as well.

00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:30.000
In all likelihood, there's another life form&nbsp;
beyond the white spikes, and perhaps the pilots,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:34.560
in the universe of "The Tomorrow War." So, while&nbsp;
humans celebrate, they might also want to cross&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:39.760
their fingers, and hope that Dan Forester is&nbsp;
around to help for longer than seven more years.&nbsp;

